  {
    "id" : "@stylistic_wrap-regex",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Wrapping of Regex Literals with Parentheses",
    "description" : "This rule requires regular expression literals to be wrapped in parentheses in certain situations to avoid ambiguity with the division operator.",
    "explanation" : "---\ntitle: wrap-regex\nrule_type: layout\n---\n\n# js/wrap-regex\n\nWhen a regular expression is used in certain situations, it can end up looking like a division operator. For example:\n\n```js\nfunction a() {\n    return /foo/.test(\"bar\");\n}\n```\n\n## Rule Details\n\nThis is used to disambiguate the slash operator and facilitates more readable code.\n\nExample of **incorrect** code for this rule:\n\n::: incorrect\n\n```js\n/*eslint wrap-regex: \"error\"*/\n\nfunction a() {\n    return /foo/.test(\"bar\");\n}\n```\n\n:::\n\nExample of **correct** code for this rule:\n\n::: correct\n\n```js\n/*eslint wrap-regex: \"error\"*/\n\nfunction a() {\n    return (/foo/).test(\"bar\");\n}\n```\n\n:::",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
