  {
    "id" : "vue_require-typed-ref",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Require typed ref in Vue",
    "description" : "Enforce that Vue's ref() and shallowRef() functions are called with explicit type parameters or initial values when using TypeScript.",
    "explanation" : "---\npageClass: rule-details\nsidebarDepth: 0\ntitle: vue/require-typed-ref\ndescription: require `ref` and `shallowRef` functions to be strongly typed\nsince: v9.15.0\n---\n\n# vue/require-typed-ref\n\n> require `ref` and `shallowRef` functions to be strongly typed\n\n## :book: Rule Details\n\nThis rule disallows calling `ref()` or `shallowRef()` functions without generic type parameter or an argument when using TypeScript.\n\nWith TypeScript it is easy to prevent usage of `any` by using [`noImplicitAny`](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig#noImplicitAny). Unfortunately this rule is easily bypassed with Vue `ref()` function. Calling `ref()` function without a generic parameter or an initial value leads to ref having `Ref<any>` type.\n\n<eslint-code-block :rules=\"{'vue/require-typed-ref': ['error']}\">\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { ref, shallowRef, type Ref } from 'vue'\n\n/* ✗ BAD */\nconst count = ref() // Returns Ref<any> that is not type checked\ncount.value = '50' // Should be a type error, but it is not\n\nconst count = shallowRef()\n\n/* ✓ GOOD */\nconst count = ref<number>()\nconst count = ref(0)\nconst count: Ref<number | undefined> = ref()\n</script>\n```\n\n</eslint-code-block>\n\n## :wrench: Options\n\nNothing.\n\n## :rocket: Version\n\nThis rule was introduced in eslint-plugin-vue v9.15.0\n\n## :mag: Implementation\n\n- [Rule source](https://github.com/vuejs/eslint-plugin-vue/blob/master/lib/rules/require-typed-ref.js)\n- [Test source](https://github.com/vuejs/eslint-plugin-vue/blob/master/tests/lib/rules/require-typed-ref.js)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "vue_require-valid-default-prop",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce valid default values for Vue props",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that default values for Vue component props are valid and appropriate for their declared types, particularly requiring objects and arrays to have default values returned from a factory function.",
    "explanation" : "---\npageClass: rule-details\nsidebarDepth: 0\ntitle: vue/require-valid-default-prop\ndescription: enforce props default values to be valid\nsince: v3.13.0\n---\n\n# vue/require-valid-default-prop\n\n> enforce props default values to be valid\n\n- :gear: This rule is included in all of `\"plugin:vue/vue3-essential\"`, `*.configs[\"flat/essential\"]`, `\"plugin:vue/essential\"`, `*.configs[\"flat/vue2-essential\"]`, `\"plugin:vue/vue3-strongly-recommended\"`, `*.configs[\"flat/strongly-recommended\"]`, `\"plugin:vue/strongly-recommended\"`, `*.configs[\"flat/vue2-strongly-recommended\"]`, `\"plugin:vue/vue3-recommended\"`, `*.configs[\"flat/recommended\"]`, `\"plugin:vue/recommended\"` and `*.configs[\"flat/vue2-recommended\"]`.\n\n## :book: Rule Details\n\nThis rule checks whether the default value of each prop is valid for the given type. It should report an error when default value for type `Array` or `Object` is not returned using function.\n\n<eslint-code-block :rules=\"{'vue/require-valid-default-prop': ['error']}\">\n\n```vue\n<script>\nexport default {\n  props: {\n    /* ✓ GOOD */\n    // basic type check (`null` means accept any type)\n    propA: Number,\n    // multiple possible types\n    propB: [String, Number],\n    // a number with default value\n    propD: {\n      type: Number,\n      default: 100\n    },\n    // object/array defaults should be returned from a factory function\n    propE: {\n      type: Object,\n      default() {\n        return { message: 'hello' }\n      }\n    },\n    propF: {\n      type: Array,\n      default() {\n        return []\n      }\n    },\n    /* ✗ BAD */\n    propA: {\n      type: String,\n      default: {}\n    },\n    propB: {\n      type: String,\n      default: []\n    },\n    propC: {\n      type: Object,\n      default: []\n    },\n    propD: {\n      type: Array,\n      default: []\n    },\n    propE: {\n      type: Object,\n      default: { message: 'hello' }\n    }\n  }\n}\n</script>\n```\n\n</eslint-code-block>\n\n## :wrench: Options\n\nNothing.\n\n## :books: Further Reading\n\n- [Guide - Prop Validation](https://vuejs.org/guide/components/props.html#prop-validation)\n\n## :rocket: Version\n\nThis rule was introduced in eslint-plugin-vue v3.13.0\n\n## :mag: Implementation\n\n- [Rule source](https://github.com/vuejs/eslint-plugin-vue/blob/master/lib/rules/require-valid-default-prop.js)\n- [Test source](https://github.com/vuejs/eslint-plugin-vue/blob/master/tests/lib/rules/require-valid-default-prop.js)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
